use scaly.memory.Page

; ---- the FRAME MARK ------------------------------------------------------
; Frame-granular reclamation for the DSSSL evaluator. `ProcessContext` brackets a
; whole construction rule in one region; this brackets every VM FRAME inside it
; by MARKING the region's bump pointer on entry and rewinding it on exit. The
; measurements behind the shape are in tests/dazzle/PERFORMANCE.md ("Die Frames
; GEZÄHLT", "Die Marke GEBAUT"): 82 M frames on a heavy codegen at 415 bytes
; each, 99.7 % of them under 4 KB, 4140 frames per eval bracket — so a region per
; frame is unaffordable and unnecessary, while a mark costs a store.
;
; A rewind is only sound if nothing above the mark is reachable afterwards, so
; everything here is the CONSERVATIVE test that decides it. Six conditions, all
; O(1), and a frame is rewound only when every one holds:
;
;   1. VALID     — the mark was taken in the eval bracket that is still current.
;   2. THE CHAIN — `rewind` refuses unless the mark's page is still the
;                  region's end; the pages the frame appended go back with it.
;   3. EXCLUSIVE — the mark page's exclusive-page list has not moved, so no
;                  oversized allocation put a PageNode inside the span
;                  (`rewind` checks this against the value taken at mark
;                  time and changes nothing when it moved).
;   4. RESULT    — the value the frame RETURNS does not lie in the span. This is
;                  the one escape route the frame owns, and it is an address
;                  comparison rather than a graph walk.
;   5. QUIET     — nothing PUBLISHED while the frame was running.
;   6. BACKFILL  — the region HEAD served no allocation while the frame ran.
;                  `Page.allocate` bumps the page it was CALLED ON when that
;                  page still has room, and every `ELObj.make_*` calls it on the
;                  head — so the frontier is not the tail alone. See the block
;                  above `below_sum`; this is the condition the memo seam's three
;                  wrong codegens were pointing at.
;
; ★★★Condition 5 is the whole trick, and it is ONE global counter. A frame's
; lifetime is exactly the window in which only IT and its DESCENDANTS run (a
; sibling runs after the pop), and every ancestor's span CONTAINS this frame's.
; So "no publication happened anywhere between my push and my pop" is sufficient
; in the conservative reading, and needs no propagation to ancestors — just
; `epoch` against `epoch_at_push`.
;
; ★★A publication is a store that could put a pointer into a marked span into
; something that survives it. The audited list: the three `SetBox` variants, a
; control-stack unwind (call/cc), a frame popped without a reported result, and
; the four lazy MEMOISATIONS the hot primitives reach (`force_select`,
; `concat_fill`, the string rope's flatten, the grove's view arrays). `store`
; decides each by TARGET — in another region it can never receive such a pointer,
; and inside the running frame's own span it dies together with it.
;
; ★★★The class that made the audit worth doing: the engine's `Page.get(object)`
; idiom protects BRACKET granularity — object region and caller scratch are
; different regions — but NOT a frame mark, because within ONE region
; `Page.get(x)` resolves to the region's END, which is above every live mark.
; That holds for every container grown off a PASSED-IN container.
;
; ★★★ON by default since 2026-08-07 — and what that decision rests on, stated
; so nobody has to reconstruct it:
;
;   PROVEN, in the sense that a counter-example would have shown up:
;     * conditions 1-4 are local arguments about addresses this frame owns;
;     * condition 6's O(1) form was cross-checked against its GENERAL form
;       (sum over every page below the mark, `DZ_FRAME_MARK_BACKFILL=N`, 27x
;       slower) with ZERO missed cases: 260 860 rewinds exhaustively on one
;       codegen and 4105 strided on the 1804-file one;
;     * differentially: 48 real-world codegens, 469 ESIS models, three backends
;       on the DocBook corpus and every suite, in BOTH engine modes.
;
;   NOT PROVEN, and this is the residual risk: condition 5's completeness. It
;   rests on a SWEEP over the engine's store sites (all 29 `Page.get(` sites
;   plus the mutating calls a primitive can reach). A store nobody hooked is
;   invisible to every gate until an output differs, and this class HAS bitten
;   once — the memo seam, where the fault turned out to be the allocator
;   (condition 6) rather than a store. A runtime scan cannot close it either:
;   a constantly rewound region is full of dead words no scan can tell from
;   live pointers, so the OUTPUT is the only oracle. The proof belongs in the
;   compiler, as the escape analysis of ROADMAP-dazzle.md's "Memory model" —
;   which exists now (dazzle/Escape.scaly) but proves 3.6 % of the bytes
;   frame-local against this mark's 38 %, so it cannot stand in for it.
;
; ⇒ The escape hatch is therefore load-bearing, not decoration:
; `DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK=0` or `--no-frame-mark` turns the mark off (and with it the
; memo seam, which follows it). If a tree ever comes out wrong, that switch is
; both the workaround and the first half of the diagnosis. tests/dazzle/
; framemark/run.sh gates the OFF path, because ON is now what every other suite
; runs. (The bisecting and verifying instruments — DZ_FRAME_MARK_LIMIT, SCAN,
; TRAP, TRAP_POP, WATCH, VERIFY, BACKFILL and the stats — were removed with the
; port's close on 2026-09-19; CLAUDE-history.md has what they found.)
; Measured on the heaviest codegen, with the memo seam that follows the mark
; (Interpreter.memo_host): peak footprint 5968 -> 3463 MB (-42.0 %), max RSS
; 5310 -> 3154 MB (-40.6 %), 17.22 GB reclaimed, cpu -0.6 % (noise).
;
; ★What was tried and MEASURED AWAY — do not rebuild it: a second arena for
; frame-local values with the escaping result placed in the eval region. Sound
; only if no argument of the escaping call lives in the frame arena, and that
; collapses, because a value produced by a non-tail call taints every consumer:
; 13 754 of 14 028 tail calls stayed behind and the win went negative. Breaking
; that cycle needs to know at the PRODUCER whether its value escapes, which is
; the dataflow and not a runtime test ("Sprosse 3 GEBAUT und VERWORFEN").
;
; ★★★And a SECOND one, measured away on 2026-08-07 — do not rebuild this either:
; a granularity heuristic that skips the mark where it does not pay ("mark only
; from the Nth frame of an eval bracket", `DZ_FRAME_MARK_MIN`, built and
; swept). It is perfectly SOUND — not marking a frame can only cost yield — and
; it is still worthless, because it treats the wrong half of the cost:
;
;   styling, 1.06 M frames | instructions | reclaimed
;   mark off               |     7.004 G  |        —
;   mark on, NOTHING marked|     7.078 G  |      0 B     <- the HOOK alone
;   mark on                |     7.091 G  |   9.56 MB
;
; ★★★So 85 % of what the mark costs that workload is being CALLED — the wrapper,
; the call, the entry, the pop's condition 1 — and only 15 % is the mark. On the
; heaviest codegen the same experiment says 70 % hook / 30 % mark (5.24 of
; 7.48 G), and there the marking half buys 14.25 GB, i.e. 6.4 bytes per
; instruction. The best threshold found recovered 3 % of the styling cost while
; giving up 77 % of the yield, and the knob cost 0.13 % just by being asked.
; ⇒ Whoever wants this back has to move the decision to the CALL SITE, and then
; owes a proof that push and pop can never disagree about it: a frame whose push
; skipped and whose pop did not reads a STALE column, and a stale column with a
; live generation is a rewind to a bogus mark. That is the corruption class this
; whole file is written to avoid, for 1 % of one workload.
;
; ---- the SHAPE of this file, which is a measurement ----------------------
;
; ★★★The mechanism costs 173 instructions per frame on the heaviest codegen
; (14.19 G for 81 775 653 frames) where its design says "one store". The
; decomposition that explains the gap is in PERFORMANCE.md ("Die Marke
; ZERLEGT"), and two of its findings are BUILT INTO the code below — read
; them before "simplifying" any of it back:
;
;   1. ONE STATE BLOCK, not eighteen globals. AArch64 materialises an `adrp`
;      per GLOBAL, not per 4 KB page: every one of these globals lives in the
;      same page and the hot path still paid ~25 `adrp` per frame. So the whole
;      hot state — the scalars AND the per-frame columns — sits in one calloc'd
;      block behind `fm_s`, read once into a register (`let f fm_s`) and
;      addressed by the FM_*/FC_* word offsets below. A new hot field goes in
;      the block.
;   2. The per-frame columns are ONE 64-byte line per frame (FM_COLS = 8), not
;      seven parallel arrays. Same instruction count for the stores, one cache
;      line instead of seven.
;   The instruments that used to hang off a probe word in the block are gone,
;   and with them the printers the inliner kept pulling into `push_on`. Check
;   the hot prologue after any change here:
;      objdump -d <bin> | sed -n '/<__ZN9FrameMark7push_onEi>:/,/ret/p'
;   No `stp`, and no `bl` outside a cold branch.

function getenv(name: pointer[const_char]) returns pointer[const_char] extern
function calloc(count: size_t, size: size_t) returns pointer[void] extern

; deeper frames are counted, not marked
define FM_MAX_DEPTH: i64 65536

; --- the state block, by word offset --------------------------------------
; scalars first, then FM_MAX_DEPTH frames of FM_COLS words each
define FM_HEAD: i64 0       ; the region being marked (the eval bracket's)
define FM_GEN: i64 1        ; its generation — condition 1
define FM_EPOCH: i64 2      ; the publication counter — condition 5
define FM_TOP: i64 3        ; the innermost live frame's index
define FM_REWOUND: i64 4    ; rewinds done — `--frame-mark-stats`, the gate's proof
define FM_HDR: i64 32       ; where the per-frame columns start

; one 64-byte line per frame
define FM_COLS: i64 8
define FC_PAGE: i64 0       ; the mark's page
define FC_OFF: i64 1        ; the mark itself
define FC_EXCL: i64 2       ; the mark page's exclusive list — condition 3
define FC_HEXCL: i64 3      ; the region HEAD's, a DIFFERENT list
define FC_HEADAT: i64 4     ; the head's bump at the push — condition 6
define FC_GEN: i64 5        ; -1 means "this frame carries no mark"
define FC_EPOCH: i64 6

; 0 = not asked yet, 1 = on, 2 = off. Its own global: the thin wrappers below
; are inlined into their callers, where the block pointer is not live.
shared fm_state: int 0
shared fm_s: pointer[i64] null

define FrameMark
{
    procedure on() returns bool mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if fm_state = 0
            FrameMark.setup()
        fm_state = 1
    }

    ; ★ON by default since 2026-08-07; `DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK=0` (or the CLI's
    ; `--no-frame-mark`) is the OPT-OUT, and it is meant to be used: if a tree
    ; ever comes out wrong, that switch is the answer, and the difference it
    ; makes is the diagnosis. Anything else, including the variable being unset,
    ; turns the mark on. `FrameMark.force_off` short-circuits this whole
    ; procedure and must therefore run before the first frame push.
    procedure setup() mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        set fm_state: 2
        let e getenv("DAZZLE_FRAME_MARK")
        if e <> null
        {
            let zero "0" as char
            if (*e as u32) = 0
                return
            if (*e as u32) = (zero as u32)
                return
        }
        ; ONE block for the whole hot state — see finding 1 in the header.
        set fm_s: calloc((FM_HDR + (FM_MAX_DEPTH * FM_COLS)) as size_t, 8) as pointer[i64]
        if fm_s = null
            return
        set fm_state: 1
    }

    ; The CLI's `--no-frame-mark`. Setting the state to "off" before anything
    ; asks makes `on()` skip `setup` entirely, which is why this is a state
    ; write and not a flag `setup` would read: it has to beat the lazy init.
    procedure force_off() mutable fm_state
        set fm_state: 2

    ; --- the region, read and written by documented OFFSET ------------------
    ;
    ; ★★★Why by offset and not through a `Page.mark`/`Page.rewind` pair in the
    ; stdlib, which is where this knowledge belongs: the planner is
    ; DEMAND-DRIVEN, so a stdlib function nothing in the COMPILER calls is never
    ; emitted into the compiler image, and the `--jit` leg of tests/dazzle/run.sh
    ; materialises this package INSIDE the compiler process and resolves its
    ; externals there (`Failed to materialize symbols: { _ZN4Page6rewindE… }`).
    ; **A new stdlib entry point may only be called from a package that the
    ; --jit leg exercises if the compiler itself also reaches it.** So the four
    ; slots are read here, against the comment in `Page.scaly` that declares them
    ; load-bearing by number:
    ;
    ;   0 next_object   1 current_page   2 next_page   3 exclusive_pages.head
    ;
    ; `Page.get` and `Page.release_page` ARE used by the compiler and therefore
    ; resolve — those two stay ordinary calls.

    function slot(p: i64, i: int) returns i64
        *((p as pointer[i64]) + i)

    ; the END of the marked region's extension chain
    function tail(head: i64) returns i64
    {
        let cp FrameMark.slot(head, 1)
        if cp = 0
            return head
        cp
    }

    ; --- hooks --------------------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; ★Each hook is a THIN wrapper around its `*_on` core, and that shape is
    ; load-bearing for the OFF path (the escape hatch). The check used to sit at
    ; the top of the big core, so every frame push, every frame pop and every
    ; store site paid a CALL to find out that the mechanism is off — measured
    ; 2026-08-07 at 2.0 % of a codegen run (`FrameMark::push` 1.10 %,
    ; `::pop` 0.80 %, `::store` 0.09 % of self time, with the mark OFF). Split
    ; this way, LTO inlines the wrapper into its callers and the off path is a
    ; load of `fm_state` plus a branch.

    ; VM.push_frame, after csp was incremented: the new frame's index is csp-1
    procedure push(csp: int) mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.push_on(csp)
    }

    procedure pop(csp: int, result: pointer[void]) mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.pop_on(csp, result)
    }

    procedure pop_unknown() mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.publish_on()
    }

    procedure store(target: pointer[void]) mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.store_on(target)
    }

    procedure publish() mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if FrameMark.on()
            FrameMark.publish_on()
    }

    procedure push_on(csp: int) mutable fm_s
    {
        let f fm_s
        let idx (csp - 1) as i64
        set *(f + FM_TOP): idx
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
            return
        let head *(f + FM_HEAD)
        let c f + (FM_HDR + (idx * FM_COLS))
        if head = 0
        {
            ; outside any eval bracket the run page is the host, and that is
            ; never rewound
            set *(c + FC_GEN): 0 - 1
            return
        }
        let t FrameMark.tail(head)
        let b FrameMark.slot(t, 0)
        if b = 0
        {
            set *(c + FC_GEN): 0 - 1
            return
        }
        set *(c + FC_PAGE): t
        set *(c + FC_OFF): b
        set *(c + FC_EXCL): FrameMark.slot(t, 3)
        set *(c + FC_HEXCL): FrameMark.slot(head, 3)
        set *(c + FC_HEADAT): FrameMark.slot(head, 0)
        set *(c + FC_GEN): *(f + FM_GEN)
        set *(c + FC_EPOCH): *(f + FM_EPOCH)
    }

    ; The ReturnInsn arm and PrimitiveObj::tailCall, before VM.pop_frame — `csp`
    ; is still the leaving frame's, so its index is csp-1, and `result` is the
    ; value it yields. ★89 % of frames leave through the tail-call path, not
    ; through the Return insn: a DSSSL body typically ends in a primitive call in
    ; tail position, and treating that path as "no result reported" makes this
    ; whole mechanism report zero.
    procedure pop_on(csp: int, result: pointer[void]) mutable fm_s
    {
        let f fm_s
        let idx (csp - 1) as i64
        set *(f + FM_TOP): idx - 1
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
            return
        let head *(f + FM_HEAD)
        if head = 0
            return
        let c f + (FM_HDR + (idx * FM_COLS))
        ; 1. VALID
        if *(c + FC_GEN) <> *(f + FM_GEN)
            return
        ; 5. QUIET — cheapest and the most common no
        if *(c + FC_EPOCH) <> *(f + FM_EPOCH)
            return
        ; 4. RESULT
        if FrameMark.in_span(c, head, result)
            return
        ; 6. BACKFILL — the region head served nothing while the frame ran.
        ; ★Only when the head is NOT the mark's page: on a single-page region the
        ; head's bump IS the span's, so testing it there refuses everything
        ; (measured: 92 refusals instead of 3, and the 89 are all this case).
        if *(c + FC_PAGE) <> head
        {
            if FrameMark.slot(head, 0) <> *(c + FC_HEADAT)
                return
        }
        ; 2. THE CHAIN and 3. EXCLUSIVE both live in `rewind`, which changes
        ; nothing when it cannot honour the mark. The count is the one number
        ; that shows the mark engaged: a silently disabled mark passes every
        ; differential there is.
        if FrameMark.rewind(f, c, head)
            set *(f + FM_REWOUND): *(f + FM_REWOUND) + 1
    }

    ; the rewinds done so far (0 with the mark off)
    function rewound() returns i64
    {
        if fm_s = null
            return 0
        *(fm_s + FM_REWOUND)
    }

    ; --- condition 6: BACKFILL ------------------------------------------------
    ;
    ; ★★★The store the sweep never hooked was not a store at all — it is the
    ; ALLOCATOR. `Page.allocate` serves from the page it was CALLED ON whenever
    ; that page still has room, and the engine calls it on the region HEAD
    ; (`host.allocate` in every `ELObj.make_*`) and on arbitrary pages of the
    ; chain (the `Page.get(object)` / `^this` / container-growth idiom). So the
    ; frontier is NOT the tail alone: a page below the mark that kept a few free
    ; bytes serves small requests forever, BELOW every live mark. Measured on the
    ; reproducer, that turned 150 characters of generated output into garbage in
    ; one file of 278.
    ;
    ; The test that ships is ONE comparison: the region HEAD's bump pointer must
    ; be the same at the pop as it was at the push. Bump pointers only move
    ; forward and no page before the mark's is released while the frame runs, so
    ; equality means the head served nothing during the window. A MIDDLE page can
    ; only become `this` through `Page.get(object)` on a PRE-EXISTING object —
    ; precisely the set of sites the store sweep hooks, so such an allocation
    ; already publishes and is stopped by condition 5. The general test (sum the
    ; bumps of every page below the mark) costs 27x and refused nothing more
    ; across the corpus; it was the verifier until the port's close.

    ; is `v` inside the span the frame at `c` marked — its page from the mark
    ; onward, plus every page appended after it?
    function in_span(c: pointer[i64], head: i64, v: pointer[void]) returns bool
    {
        if v = null
            return false
        let mp *(c + FC_PAGE)
        let vp Page.get(v) as i64
        if vp = mp
        {
            if (v as i64) >= *(c + FC_OFF)
                return true
            return false
        }
        ; another region can never be in the span: a container growing off an
        ; object there allocates at THAT region's end
        if FrameMark.slot(vp, 4) <> head
            return false
        ; same region — only the pages AFTER the mark's page are in the span, and
        ; a frame appends none in 99.7 % of cases, so this walk is usually empty
        var p FrameMark.slot(mp, 2)
        while p <> 0
        {
            if p = vp
                return true
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        false
    }

    ; Give back everything allocated in the marked region since the frame's mark.
    ; Answers false and changes NOTHING when the mark cannot be honoured.
    ;
    ; Two passes on purpose: VALIDATE the chain first, then release. A page whose
    ; own exclusive-page list grew since the mark must not be dropped half way,
    ; and `allocate_oversized` attaches to the page it was CALLED on rather than
    ; to the head, so the check is per page.
    procedure rewind(mutable f: pointer[i64], c: pointer[i64], head: i64) returns bool
    {
        let mp *(c + FC_PAGE)
        let mark *(c + FC_OFF)
        ; NO "the mark's page must still be the end" test — that was the
        ; single-page restriction, and dropping it is the point: pages appended
        ; after `mp` are exactly what this frame added, because `allocate` only
        ; ever appends at the region's end.
        ; an oversized page has no bump pointer and cannot carry a mark
        let now FrameMark.slot(mp, 0)
        if now = 0
            return false
        if mark < mp
            return false
        if mark > now
            return false
        ; the mark page's own exclusive list must not have moved: an oversized
        ; allocation since the mark put its PageNode inside the span, so the
        ; surviving list head would dangle
        if FrameMark.slot(mp, 3) <> *(c + FC_EXCL)
            return false
        ; ★★And the region HEAD's list as well, which is a DIFFERENT list as soon
        ; as the mark sits on an extension page. `allocate` forwards an oversized
        ; request to `allocate_oversized` on the page it was CALLED on, and a
        ; frame allocates on its host — the head. Its PageNode is then
        ; bump-allocated at the region's end, i.e. inside the span, so the
        ; surviving head's list would dangle. Leaving this out cost 3 of the 48
        ; real-world codegens; the gate is what found it.
        if FrameMark.slot(head, 3) <> *(c + FC_HEXCL)
            return false
        ; the pages this frame APPENDED go back too. It is not the frame's size
        ; that makes that necessary — 99.7 % allocate under 4 KB — but where the
        ; bump pointer happened to stand at entry; measured, it decides 19 % of
        ; all frames. VALIDATE the whole chain first: a page whose own exclusive
        ; list is non-empty must not be dropped (its PageNode sits in the span we
        ; are about to reclaim), and noticing that half way through the release
        ; would leave the region in pieces.
        var p FrameMark.slot(mp, 2)
        var pages: i64 0
        while p <> 0
        {
            if FrameMark.slot(p, 3) <> 0
                return false
            set pages: pages + 1
            set p: FrameMark.slot(p, 2)
        }
        if now = mark
        {
            if pages = 0
                return true
        }
        var q FrameMark.slot(mp, 2)
        while q <> 0
        {
            let following FrameMark.slot(q, 2)
            Page.release_page(q as pointer[Page])
            set q: following
        }
        let w mp as pointer[i64]
        ; cut the chain and make the mark's page the end again. The head records
        ; the end, and null means "the head IS the end" — the state `allocate`
        ; expects, it appends on demand.
        set *(w + 2): 0
        let hw head as pointer[i64]
        if mp = head
            set *(hw + 1): 0
        else
            set *(hw + 1): mp
        set *w: mark
        true
    }

    procedure publish_on() mutable fm_s
    {
        let f fm_s
        set *(f + FM_EPOCH): *(f + FM_EPOCH) + 1
    }

    ; ★A STORE into a pre-existing object. It publishes in exactly one shape:
    ; the target lives in the region being marked AND below the innermost frame's
    ; mark — then whatever the store allocates lands at the region's END, inside
    ; the span, while the target survives the rewind. A target in another region
    ; (the grove, the run page) allocates at THAT region's end and can never
    ; point into the span; a target inside the span dies with it.
    procedure store_on(target: pointer[void]) mutable fm_s
    {
        if target = null
            return
        let f fm_s
        let head *(f + FM_HEAD)
        if head = 0
            return
        let tp Page.get(target) as i64
        if FrameMark.slot(tp, 4) <> head
            return
        let top *(f + FM_TOP)
        if top < 0
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        if top >= FM_MAX_DEPTH
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        let c f + (FM_HDR + (top * FM_COLS))
        if *(c + FC_GEN) <> *(f + FM_GEN)
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        ; ★★★Deliberately STRICTER than `in_span`: the target counts as safe only
        ; when it sits on the innermost frame's own MARK PAGE at or above the
        ; mark, and the region has not moved off that page since. Reasoning that
        ; a target on a LATER page of the span is equally safe is what the
        ; rewritten version did — and it made 3 of the 48 real-world codegens
        ; differ while publishing 42 000 times less. The looser rule may well be
        ; defensible; it is not GATED, and this one is.
        let t FrameMark.tail(head)
        if t <> *(c + FC_PAGE)
        {
            FrameMark.publish_on()
            return
        }
        let r target as i64
        if r >= *(c + FC_OFF)
        {
            if r < FrameMark.slot(t, 0)
                return
        }
        FrameMark.publish_on()
    }

    ; Interpreter.set_eval_region — the region under every live mark changes
    procedure region(p: ref[Page]?) mutable fm_s, fm_state
    {
        if FrameMark.on() = false
            return
        let f fm_s
        if (p as i64) = *(f + FM_HEAD)
            return
        set *(f + FM_HEAD): p as i64
        set *(f + FM_GEN): *(f + FM_GEN) + 1
    }
}
